  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A surface trough over central NC will push east\nacross ENC this afternoon, and will interact with the seabreeze\nand a moderately unstable airmass. Despite some drier air \nmoving in aloft, there should be sufficient low-level \nconvergence to support isolated to scattered convection through \nearly this evening. The deepest convection should be focused \neast of HWY 17 where low-level forcing will be strongest. Deep \nlayer shear will be weak (~20kt) and this should help to \nminimize the risk of strong to severe thunderstorms. \nAdditionally, weak low-level lapse rates and low DCAPE should \nlimit the magnitude of gusts with any of the taller cores. With \nnotable PWATs in place, moderate to high rainfall rates will be \npossible in the strongest thunderstorms. Westerly flow in \nopposition of the seabreeze may allow thunderstorms in that area\nto have more residence time due to a net motion that is very \nslow. Should this occur, a localized flood risk may develop. \nHowever, with drier air trying to work in aloft, confidence in \nsustained deep convection is lower, and the overall flood risk \nappears low for the area at large. \n\nOn Monday, a cold front will move south along the coast of NC \nduring peak heating. West to northwest low-level flow should \ntend to limit the strength of convergence along the front. \nHowever, as the front encounters the developing seabreeze, there\nshould be a local enhancement to low-level forcing, especially \nalong and south of HWY 70. This is the area where the best \nchance for sustained, deep convection exists. The risk is \nconditional on sufficient low-level forcing, but should this \noccur, a short-lived risk of strong to marginally severe \nthunderstorms may develop thanks to elevated deep layer shear \n(~30kt) and moderate instability (MLCAPE up to 1500j/kg). \nMachine learning guidance isn't all that excited about this \npotential, and this likely speaks to the conditionality of the \nthreat. This risk will quickly end from north to south as the \nfront shifts off the Crystal Coast by sunset.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance continue to trend stronger with an \nupper level ridge that will be expanding across the Southern \nU.S. this week. In fact, ensemble guidance is now forecasting \n500mb heights around 600dm with this ridge, the strength of \nwhich is very unusual for this time of year. Normally, this \nwould spell anomalous heat for ENC. However, a pocket of \n\"cooler\" thicknesses is forecast to be over the Carolinas \nassociated with the post-frontal northeasterly flow regime, \nespecially Tuesday and Wednesday. While it will be dry, those \ncooler thicknesses may shield ENC from the anomalous heat that \nother parts of the South will experience. That pocket of lower \nthicknesses may begin to erode by the end of the week, with \ntemperatures warming by then. However, guidance differ on \nwhen/if this will occur.",
